  "kind": "analysis",
  "analysis": {
    "details": [
      "A spike is the session a symbol's put/call volume ratio first closes two or more standard deviations above its own trailing 20-day mean. The bias is contrarian: heavy put buying is read as fear that is about to wash out.",
      "On the first, five-stock version of this package it was the one signal of 21 to clear |t| ≥ 2. AAPL, AMD, NVDA and TSLA are still here, and on them it still does: 111 firings, 63% up five sessions later against 55% normally, +1.41 points over base.",
      "Over the 29 symbols added since, 720 firings averaged +0.21 points (t 0.5). 18 of all 33 symbols show a positive edge and 15 a negative one, about the split chance gives.",
      "Pooled, the signal reads +0.37 points at t 1.1: Noise. The earlier result is what testing 21 signals on five names should be expected to throw up once. That is the reason to re-run a finding on symbols it was not found on before acting on it."
    ],
